{"name":"Del Sol Maize","hex":"#dabf92","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"del","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Delaware languages."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The symbol ∇ used to denote the gradient operator.","the symbol ∂, in the context of a partial differential"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/del"},{"term":"sol","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Solos."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["In a movable-do or tonic sol-fa system: the fifth step in a scale, preceded by fa and followed by la.\n\nIn a fixed-do system: the musical note G.","In a movable-do or tonic sol-fa system: the fifth step in a scale, preceded by fa and followed by la."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sol"},{"term":"maize","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Corn; a type of grain of the species Zea mays."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/maize"}],"entries":[{"title":"Del","blocks":[{"text":"Del, or nabla, is an operator used in mathematics as a vector differential operator, usually represented by ∇. When applied to a function defined on a one-dimensional domain, it denotes the standard derivative of the function as defined in calculus. When applied to a field, it may denote any one of three operations depending on the way it is applied: the gradient or (locally) steepest slope of a scalar field ; the divergence of a vector field; or the curl (rotation) of a vector field."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Del","wikidata":"Q334508"},{"title":"Maize","blocks":[{"text":"Maize, also known as corn, is a tall stout grass that produces cereal grain. The leafy stalk of the plant gives rise to male inflorescences or tassels which produce pollen, and female inflorescences called ears. The ears yield grain, known as kernels or seeds. In modern commercial varieties, these are usually yellow or white; other varieties can be of many colors. Maize was domesticated by indigenous peoples in southern Mexico about 9,000 years ago from wild teosinte. Native Americans planted it alongside beans and squashes in the Three Sisters polyculture."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg/330px-Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maize","wikidata":"Q11575"},{"title":"Maize (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The shade maize or corn refers to a specific tone of yellow; it is named for the cereal of the same name—maize. In public usage, maize can be applied to a variety of shades, ranging from light yellow to a dark shade that borders on orange, since the color of maize may vary."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Cornheap.jpg/330px-Cornheap.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maize_(color)","wikidata":"Q3843568"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg/330px-Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg/330px-Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKoeh-283.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Corntassel_7095.jpg/250px-Corntassel_7095.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Corntassel_7095.jpg/330px-Corntas